{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3244","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-process-siren-diagram-disclosure.md@sha256:e140b9940035ee837d3375c89cc2dd0c4e181abffd9c8a737d63f9547fa5586c"},"owner_reference":"3244","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nprocess-siren avoids prose/diagram duplication and auto-discloses large diagrams\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nprocess-siren (`plugin-creator:process-siren` agent) and `process-siren:woo-sailor` (skill) always inline the Mermaid diagrams they produce into the target file, and treat the diagram as a \"Reference\" cross-check that must be kept in sync with parallel prose describing the same process. This causes two compounding problems, observed concretely on `plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` across PRs #3240, #3241, #3243, #3245, #3247 this session: (1) token bloat \u2014 a full-fidelity diagram rebuild (PR #3243) pushed the file from 4306 to 4977 tokens, over skilllint's SK006/AS005 4400-token warning threshold; (2) duplication \u2014 the file's numbered Steps 1-7 prose fully restates the same branching/sequencing the diagram encodes, which is real \"same meaning in more than one place\" duplication per this repo's `mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents` convention, not just a size problem (tracked as the concrete instance in backlog item #3246).\n\nAgents consume Mermaid as notation \u2014 they parse the diagram's text directly and never render it visually \u2014 so a diagram's value is branching precision and notation density for an agent reader, not visual communication. That means prose duplicating the same logic a diagram already encodes adds no signal an agent needs, at real, recurring token cost every time process-siren is run on a skill with existing step-by-step prose.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. process-siren/woo-sailor never leaves a target file with both a diagram and prose that duplicate the same branching/sequencing logic \u2014 the diagram becomes the sole authoritative representation of sequencing; prose is limited to supplementary detail the notation cannot carry (exact call signatures, payload/field shapes, schema pointers).\n2. When a diagram is reference/verification material that not every execution path needs, and inlining it would push the target file over its governing token-budget threshold, process-siren discloses it to a `references/*.md` file reached by a context pointer, rather than leaving it inline.\n3. A diagram that is load-bearing for every execution path stays inline regardless of size \u2014 disclosure is never applied purely because a diagram is large.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Retrofitting every existing skill in this repo that currently has prose/diagram duplication is out of scope here \u2014 this item changes process-siren's own future-run behavior only. The one already-identified concrete instance (`multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md`) is tracked separately as backlog item #3246.\n2. Designing a general-purpose \"skill size/duplication linter,\" or extending `skilllint`'s own threshold logic, is out of scope \u2014 this item only changes what process-siren produces, not what skilllint checks.\n3. A bare `.mmd`/`.mermaid` extracted-diagram file is out of scope \u2014 any extracted diagram must follow this repo's existing `references/*.md` + context-pointer convention (working precedent: `references/acceptance-test-guide.md`, `references/dispatch-flow.md` in the same skill).\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. When process-siren/woo-sailor is invoked against a target file that already has prose describing the same sequencing/branching the diagram it produces will encode, it resolves that duplication (removes or replaces the duplicating prose, or clearly flags it for the invoking user/agent to resolve) rather than leaving both the diagram and the prose in place.\n2. When a diagram process-siren produces is reference/verification material rather than something every execution path needs, and inlining it would push the target file over its token-budget threshold, process-siren creates a `references/*.md` file containing the diagram and replaces the inline diagram with a context-pointer sentence in the target file (front-loaded trigger wording, stating the branch/condition that reaches it \u2014 per `mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents` pointer guidance).\n3. When a diagram is load-bearing for every execution path, process-siren keeps it inline even when large.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. The extraction/de-duplication decision must be visible, not silent \u2014 a human or downstream agent must be able to see what was moved or removed, where it went, and why (matches this repo's existing convention of reporting a disposition per changed item).\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n<!-- Route user-supplied design proposals here, not to Requirements.\n     Each entry is a signal for the architect's research phase, not a commitment. -->\n\n1. User proposed tying the extraction threshold to skilllint's own 4400-token SK006/AS005 line (or a diagram-specific line count) \u2014 evaluate whether that is the right trigger, versus a diagram-specific threshold, versus the clarity/load-bearing-ness heuristic surfaced later in the same design conversation (see Resolved Questions \u2014 the clarity-based framing supersedes pure token-count as the *primary* trigger; token count is corroborating evidence).\n2. User proposed updating \"the `plugin-creator:process-siren` agent definition and/or the `process-siren:woo-sailor` skill's dispatch logic\" \u2014 evaluate which file(s) actually need to change, and whether both.\n3. How should process-siren detect and resolve \"prose already duplicates this diagram\" for a pre-existing file it did not itself just create the diagram in (versus only guarding against *introducing new* duplication when it authors a fresh diagram) \u2014 is retrofit detection in scope for this item, or does it require a separate audit/lint pass? (Backlog item #3246 is the one concrete instance already identified.)\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` and `references/dispatch-flow.md` (this repo) \u2014 the concrete instance that surfaced this problem, across PRs #3240, #3241, #3243, #3245, #3247.\n- `mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents` skill \u2014 progressive disclosure and duplication principles this item is grounded in.\n- `dh:work-backlog-item`'s routing sections (`plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md`) \u2014 existing working precedent in this repo for \"diagram only, zero parallel prose.\"\n- Backlog item #3246 \u2014 the concrete convergence fix for `multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` specifically; related but separately scoped.\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Is the extraction trigger primarily about token count, or about whether the diagram is load-bearing for every execution path? | Primarily whether the diagram is load-bearing for every path (serves clarity); a token-threshold crossing is corroborating evidence, not the primary trigger. | User, this session |\n| Do agents visually render the diagram, or read it as notation? | Agents read Mermaid as notation directly \u2014 they never see a rendered picture. This is why prose duplicating the same logic is pure waste, not a communication aid. | User, this session |\n| Should a diagram and prose ever both describe the same branching logic? | No \u2014 that is the duplication anti-pattern to eliminate. The diagram should be the sole authoritative sequencing/branching source. | User, this session |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- None identified as blocking. The Design Questions above are inputs to planning/architecture research, not decisions requiring human resolution before proceeding.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [ ] User confirms this document captures their intent\n"}